Directive 02a (2026-09-15) - the role model. Documents only; 205 tests pass
untouched.

The closed four-role literal is retired. Contract section 4 now owns
authorization: permissions as the unit of "may do this", roles as editable
data bundles over a permission catalogue assembled from app manifests,
default-deny against effective permissions, the four existing names reframed
as the shipped default bundles rather than deleted.

The reasoning worth keeping: the security lab's "a permission, not a fifth
role" wording from Directive 02 was not a neat workaround, it was evidence the
model failed on first extension. Every later authority would have needed the
same carve-out.

Authentication and authorization are now separate axes in the document -
section 5.1 owns sign-in shape and account derivation, section 4 owns what an
identity may do. That split is what lets a passwordless profile hold any
bundle, which matters most at the minimum spec where passwordless is the
normal case.

Deliberately NOT done: no closed permission catalogue. Representative examples
only. Freezing the list here would have rebuilt the same rigidity one layer
down, and the directive said so.

Closed: H13 = Community broadcast (H11 owns audio as content, H13 owns the
station). community_hub/field_ops do not require x86_64 - the floor is the
resources, so a 16 GB ARM64 box qualifies.

Still outstanding and unchanged: the Directive 01 rig work. The read-only
SQLite VFS has still only ever run on Windows. Next implementation directive
carries TWO migrations sequenced together - min_hw_tier and the role enum -
because both are code catching up to documented models on overlapping surfaces.
